{# DNOS OSPF.

   OSPFv2 AND OSPFv3 LIVE UNDER DIFFERENT TOP-LEVEL NODES, and this is the thing to get right.
   OSPFv2 is "protocols ospf" holding one or more named INSTANCES. OSPFv3 is "protocols ospfv3",
   a singleton: there is no "protocols ospfv3 instance <x>" path anywhere in the CLI model
   ("cmd search ospfv3 | no-more" lists every path it has), and no second OSPFv3 process.

   Rendering OSPFv3 as "protocols ospf / instance ospfv3" reads plausibly and the CLI ACCEPTS
   it -- but it does not configure OSPFv3. It configures a second OSPFv2 instance that happens
   to be named "ospfv3", holding interfaces that carry no IPv4 addresses. Nothing says so
   directly; what gives it away is DNOS's uniqueness check on administrative distances, which
   only ever compares instances of the SAME protocol:

       ERROR: OSPF 'administrative-distance-external' in instance 'ospf' is the same as
              'administrative-distance-external' in instance 'ospfv3'.

   A dual-stack node cannot commit at all, and a single-stack IPv6 node commits an OSPFv2
   instance that never forms an adjacency. Found by deploying to cDNOS; it applies equally to
   the hardware.

   An address family is emitted only when it actually has interfaces. Without that check an
   IPv4-only link leaves a stranded, empty OSPFv3 process carrying a router-id and nothing else
   -- the device accepts it and keeps it, so nothing complains, but it is wrong. This is easy to
   hit on physical hardware: once the system loopback is excluded (it belongs to the device, not
   to netlab), a single-family link can be all that remains.

   The system loopback is excluded unless netlab is the owner of record. On a device in service
   the loopback anchors the BGP router-id and L2VPN route-distinguishers, so it must not be
   pulled into an OSPF area that netlab created. #}
{% set _own  = netlab_manage_identity|default(True) %}
{% set _lb   = loopback.ifname|default('') %}
{% set _dflt = ospf.area|default("0.0.0.0") %}
{#  The area/interface body is identical for the two address families and only its indentation
    differs (OSPFv2 sits one level deeper, under "instance"), so it is written once. #}
{% macro area_block(_if) %}
{% set _areas = [] %}
{% for l in _if if l.ospf.area|default(_dflt) not in _areas %}
{%   set _ = _areas.append(l.ospf.area|default(_dflt)) %}
{% endfor %}
{% for area in _areas %}
area {{ area }}
{%   for l in _if if l.ospf.area|default(_dflt) == area %}
  interface {{ l.ifname }}
{#   "passive" takes a value on DNOS ("passive enabled"); a bare "passive" is rejected with
     "Unknown word: 'passive'". So is the whole keyword on a LOOPBACK, in either address
     family -- a loopback advertises without forming adjacencies whatever we say, so DNOS
     does not offer the leaf there. netlab marks stub and loopback interfaces passive, which
     is how a config that is right everywhere else fails to load. #}
{%     if l.ospf.passive|default(False) and l.type|default('') != 'loopback' %}
    passive enabled
{%     endif %}
{%     if l.ospf.network_type|default("") == "point-to-point" %}
    network-type point-to-point
{%     endif %}
  !
{%   endfor %}
!
{% endfor %}
{% endmacro %}
{% set _if4 = [] %}
{% for l in netlab_interfaces|default([])
     if l.ospf is defined and l.ipv4 is defined and (_own or l.ifname != _lb) %}
{%   set _ = _if4.append(l) %}
{% endfor %}
{% set _if6 = [] %}
{% for l in netlab_interfaces|default([])
     if l.ospf is defined and l.ipv6 is defined and (_own or l.ifname != _lb) %}
{%   set _ = _if6.append(l) %}
{% endfor %}
protocols
{% if _if4 and ospf.af.ipv4 is defined %}
  ospf
{#  NETLAB'S OSPFv2 INSTANCE IS CALLED "ospf", AND WHAT THAT MEANS DEPENDS ON THE BOX.

    OSPFv2 on DNOS is a set of NAMED instances and "load merge" merges by name, so on a device
    netlab does not own there are two outcomes and netlab cannot tell them apart in advance:

     * the box runs OSPF under some other name -- "IPUNNUM" on the 72XC, and this is the normal
       case -- so netlab CREATES its own instance alongside the operator's. Everything under
       "COEXISTENCE" below then applies, because DNOS enforces rules ACROSS instances.
     * the box already has an instance called "ospf". Then netlab does not create anything:
       its areas and interfaces land INSIDE the operator's OSPF process. The consequence worth
       understanding before running this against a box in service is that a netlab-managed link
       then belongs to the operator's OSPF domain -- it forms adjacencies there and floods LSAs
       into it -- which is what you want when netlab is extending a fabric, and is not what you
       want if you expected an isolated lab process. In that case the three
       administrative-distance values below also land on the operator's instance and replace
       theirs; nothing else netlab renders touches an attribute the operator owns.

    Both were exercised on the physical 72XC (DNOS 26.1.0) with candidate-only dry runs, and the
    box was byte-identical afterwards each time.

    ESCAPE HATCH -- netlab_dnos_ospf_instance renames the instance, for anyone who wants a
    separate netlab-owned process on a box whose production instance IS called "ospf". Two
    things to know before reaching for it:

     * IT ONLY COVERS IPv4. OSPFv3 has no instance layer at all (see below), so on a dual-stack
       node the v4 interfaces move into the renamed instance while the v6 interfaces still merge
       into the operator's "protocols ospfv3" and flood there. The hatch does not isolate the
       node, only half of it.
     * THE OFFLINE PREVIEW DOES NOT SHOW IT. This variable, netlab_dnos_ospf_router_id and the
       three distance overrides are read from Ansible group_vars, and "netlab initial -o" (and
       every other offline render) merges DEVICE group_vars only -- never topology groups.*.vars.
       So the preview shows the DEFAULT configuration while the real deploy pushes the
       overridden one. On a production router, check the candidate diff the deploy itself
       prints, not the offline render.

    Set it the way netlab_dnos_commit is set (see tasks/deploy-config/dnos.yml):

        groups:
          all:
            vars:
              netlab_dnos_ospf_instance: netlab

    Renaming also brings the cross-instance rules into play on a box where they did not apply
    before -- router-id agreement, distinct distances, and one instance per ROUTED interface:

        ERROR: Unable to set interface 'ge100-0/0/1' under OSPF instance netlab area
               '0.0.0.0'. Interface attached to OSPF instance ospf area '0.0.0.0'.

    -- which is the right outcome anyway: it refuses to move a live routed interface out of the
    production IGP. A LOOPBACK is exempt: a second instance may take one that another instance
    already has (verified with a matching router-id and distinct distances, so nothing else
    masked it). IS-IS behaves identically -- see isis/dnos.j2, where it was measured -- so this
    is a DNOS rule about interface kinds, not something specific to OSPF. #}
{%   set _inst = netlab_dnos_ospf_instance|default('ospf') %}
    instance {{ _inst }}
{%   if _own %}
      router-id {{ ospf.router_id }}
{%   else %}
{#   COEXISTENCE WITH AN EXISTING OSPFv2 INSTANCE.

     Only reached on a device netlab does not own, which is normally running OSPF under a name
     of its own -- so netlab's instance is a SECOND instance, and DNOS constrains what a second
     instance may look like.

     ADMINISTRATIVE DISTANCES ARE WHAT MAKE THAT PATH WORK AT ALL, which is why they are
     emitted unconditionally rather than on request. DNOS requires the distances to be distinct
     ACROSS instances, and compares per-value, not per-kind -- our inter-area is checked against
     their external too -- so two instances sitting on the defaults cannot both commit. Proven
     on the 72XC, whose operator instance "IPUNNUM" carries no explicit distances: without this
     block, "commit check" refuses the candidate outright --

         ERROR: OSPF 'administrative-distance-external' in instance 'ospf' is the same as
                'administrative-distance-external' in instance 'IPUNNUM'.

     -- and with 200/201/202 it passes. Making them opt-in reads like the safer choice and is
     not: it leaves the default broken on any box that already runs OSPF, which is the normal
     case for hardware. The three overrides exist for the box where 200-202 are themselves
     taken. A clash is never silent: DNOS names the conflicting instance and attribute at
     "commit check", before anything is applied, and the external provider is a dry run by
     default.

     ROUTER-ID. Instances must agree on the CONFIGURED router-id, and DNOS compares it in BOTH
     directions: against an instance that has none, an explicit router-id is rejected whatever
     value we use; against an instance that HAS one, the omission is rejected --

         ERROR: OSPF router-id 'None' in instance 'netlab' is different from
                router-id '10.8.0.1' in instance 'ospf'

     Nothing is emitted by default because the 72XC's instance has none, and because the value
     netlab planned for its own loopback is almost certainly not the one a box in service uses.
     netlab_dnos_ospf_router_id supplies the value the error message names; read it with
     "show config protocols ospf instance <theirs>".

     None of this applies to OSPFv3 below: there is exactly ONE OSPFv3 process per device and
     no name to distinguish it by, so netlab cannot have its own and never sets a distance
     there -- doing so would be re-writing the OPERATOR'S value, not ours. #}
{%     if netlab_dnos_ospf_router_id is defined %}
      router-id {{ netlab_dnos_ospf_router_id }}
{%     endif %}
      administrative-distance external {{ netlab_dnos_ospf_distance_external|default(200) }}
      administrative-distance inter-area {{ netlab_dnos_ospf_distance_inter_area|default(201) }}
      administrative-distance intra-area {{ netlab_dnos_ospf_distance_intra_area|default(202) }}
{%   endif %}
{{ area_block(_if4)|trim|indent(6,True) }}
    !
  !
{% endif %}
{% if _if6 and ospf.af.ipv6 is defined %}
{#  There is exactly one OSPFv3 process per device. On a box netlab does not own, "load merge"
    therefore MERGES netlab's areas and interfaces into the operator's OSPFv3 rather than
    standing up anything of its own -- which is why the router-id is withheld: it is theirs. #}
  ospfv3
{%   if _own %}
    router-id {{ ospf.router_id }}
{%   endif %}
{{ area_block(_if6)|trim|indent(4,True) }}
  !
{% endif %}
!
